.TH DISBENCH 1
.SH NAME
disbench \- time the Dis virtual machine, with an answer that can be checked
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B disbench
[
.BI -n " reps"
] [
.I test ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Disbench
runs a small set of loops under the Dis virtual machine and prints, for
each one, how long it took and what it computed.  It exists to answer
whether the JIT is worth anything, which nothing in this tree could
answer before: every measurement had been a boot, and a boot runs each
module about once, which is the worst case a code generator can be asked
for.
.PP
Each test prints one line
.IP
.EX
DISBENCH \fIname\fP \fIiterations\fP \fImilliseconds\fP \fIchecksum\fP
.EE
.PP
and the last line is
.B DISBENCH-OK
or
.BR DISBENCH-FAIL ,
so a boot log can be judged without a human reading the numbers.  With no
.I test
named it runs them all.
.PP
.B -n
repeats each test's inner loop
.I reps
times.  The loop sizes are constants, so the checksum is the same at
every
.BR -n :
a longer run never has to switch the check off.  The defaults are sized
for a board under emulation; a host wants
.B -n
.B 20
or more.
.SS The tests
.TP
.B int
Shifts, xors and a backward branch, and nothing that leaves the generated
code.  Twenty-two bits of state, not thirty-two, so that a generator
using a 32-bit register and an interpreter using a 64-bit one agree \- an
int is 32 bits to the compiler and 64 at runtime in this tree, and a
recurrence that relies on wrapping would make a badly written benchmark
look like a JIT bug.
.TP
.B loop
The control for
.BR call :
the same sum over the same iterations with no call in it.
.TP
.B call
The same loop with a function call in it, so
.B call
minus
.B loop
is what a Dis call costs.
.TP
.B array
Indexed loads and stores, which carry a bounds check the generator emits
itself.
.TP
.B real
Kahan summation of 1/k, checked to a tolerance of 1e-12 against the same
algorithm run in Python.  Compensated summation in IEEE double is
deterministic \- the order is fixed by the loop and every operation is
correctly rounded \- so a conforming implementation gets the same bits,
and a tolerance a thousandth of a unit in the last place is far tighter
than any plausible code-generator bug.  The check used to be
.B "5.0 < sum < 100.0" ,
a decade wide, which would have passed through almost anything.
.TP
.B string
Allocation and copying, all of it punted to C.  It is the second control:
whatever the other tests do, this one should not move much between a JIT
and an interpreter, and if it does then the difference is in the
allocator and not in the code generator.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/disbench.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR emu (1),
.IR mprof (1),
.B doc/multicore-ship-plan.ms
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A test whose checksum is wrong prints
.B WRONG
and the expected value on its own line, and the run ends
.BR DISBENCH-FAIL .
The expected values are not recorded from a run of this program: each is
the same recurrence evaluated in Python, outside this virtual machine, so
a mismatch is the VM disagreeing with arithmetic rather than with a
previous afternoon.
.SH BUGS
It measures the virtual machine and nothing else.  There is no I/O, no
channel, no process and no garbage collection pressure in it, so it says
nothing about a real program's time, and a speedup here is an upper bound
on what a real program would see.
.PP
.B call
is only as honest as its control.  Its first version had a callee of
.B "return i" ,
which
.IR limbo (1)
inlines \- a function with no locals whose body is a single return, see
.B fninline()
in
.B limbo/typecheck.c
and the
.B -i
flag that turns it off \- so there was no call in the call test and it
came out within two per cent of
.BR loop .
The callee has a local for that reason and no other.
.PP
The numbers depend on whether the Dis word tracker is running; see
.B distrack
in
.BR libinterp/heap.c .
It is off by default and costs about ninety-six per cent of the
interpreter's time when it is on, which is enough to make a JIT look
sixty times better than it is.
